NAME
       login - begin a session on the system

SYNOPSIS
       login [-f username] [-h host] [username]

DESCRIPTION
       login authenticates a user and starts a session.  It is normally
       invoked by getty(1) on a terminal, but may also be run directly.

       When invoked with a username argument (as getty does) login prompts
       only for the password.  Otherwise it prompts for both the login name
       and the password.

       Authentication is performed through the PAM framework (see
       pam_appl.h), which verifies the supplied password against the
       user's entry in /etc/shadow using yescrypt password hashing.

       On success login:

         o  sets the real, effective and saved user and group IDs and the
            supplementary group list from /etc/passwd and /etc/group;
         o  sets the environment variables HOME, USER, LOGNAME, SHELL,
            PATH and TERM;
         o  changes to the user's home directory; and
         o  executes the user's login shell.

       The shell is started as a login shell: argv[0] is the shell's base
       name prefixed with '-' (for example "-sh"), so the shell reads its
       login startup files.

OPTIONS
       -f username
              Skip authentication and log the named user in directly.  This
              option is restricted to the superuser.

       -h host
              Record the name of the remote host.  Reserved for a future
              network login service (sshd); it has no effect at present.

FILES
       /etc/passwd    user account database
       /etc/shadow    encrypted passwords (readable only by root)
       /etc/group     group database

EXIT STATUS
       login exits nonzero if authentication fails after three attempts or
       if the account cannot be set up.  On success it does not return: it
       replaces itself with the user's shell.

SEE ALSO
       getty(1), init(1), sh(1)

AUTHORS
